    "title": "HIGH WIND WARNING: When will wind gusts die down?",
    "text": "Editor’s Note: The forecast below has been updated and is no longer active. Please click here for the latest weather conditions. (WJW) — The National Weather Service has issued a HIGH WIND WARNING for Ashland, Ashtabula, Carroll, Coshocton, Crawford, Cuyahoga, Erie, Geauga, Holmes, Huron, Lake, Lorain, Mahoning, Medina, Ottawa, Portage, Richland, Sandusky, Stark, Summit, Trumbull, Tuscarawas and Wayne counties. The warning will remain in effect as late as 2 a.m. on Saturday in some areas. Wind gusts higher than 60 mph are expected and could blow down trees and power lines. Travel will be difficult, especially for high-profile vehicles. Wind gusts could still be as high as 50 mph through midnight and above 40 mph through the overnight. An 85 mph wind gusts was recorded at Cleveland’s Burke Lakefront Airport on Friday. Akron-Canton, Cleveland Hopkins, Lorain also recorded max wind gusts above 70 mph. For comparison, an EF0 tornado produces wind gusts between 65 and 85 mph. The strong winds caused tree damage and power outages. More than 80,000 customers were without power in Cuyahoga County at one point on Friday. Other than windy, Friday night will be partly to mostly cloudy and seasonably chilly. Temperatures will bottom out on either side of 30 degrees. The gusty winds will put wind chills near 20 degrees Saturday morning. The wind will be much lighter Saturday afternoon! Overall, Saturday will be a much quieter day. High temperatures on Saturday will be in the lower to mid 40s, which is near or just below normal for this time of year. Sunday will be the warmer half of the weekend, with highs back in the 60s. Here’s a look at a few events happening this weekend: Another strong storm system will impact the area Sunday night into Monday morning. Rain showers will develop along a cold front Sunday night into Monday morning. Temperatures will start out in the 50s Monday morning then drop throughout the day as much colder air moves in behind the cold front. Temperatures will drop into the 30s Monday afternoon. Lake effect snow showers will develop Monday evening and continue through Tuesday. St. Patrick’s Day will be cold for everyone, and snowy for some. The roller coaster temperatures will continue this weekend and early next week. Temperatures will briefly go up this weekend then come crashing down early next week. The cold weather early next week will lead to lake effect snow showers late Monday into Tuesday. Temperatures will start to warm back up towards the middle of next week. Here’s the latest 8 Day Forecast: Stay up-to-date by downloading the FOX 8 apps, including the new FOX 8 CLE+ streaming app available for free on Amazon Fire, Roku, and Apple TV.",
